research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6047:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:4:title: "$\\aleph_0$ is regular in ZF; assuming the Axiom of Choice every successor aleph $\\aleph_{\\alpha+1}$ is regular; $\\operatorname{cf}(\\aleph_\\omega) = \\aleph_0$, so $\\aleph_\\omega$ is singular, and under choice it is the least singular infinite cardinal"
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6048:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:10:deps: [thm-cofinality-basics, def-cofinality, lem-cofinality-is-well-defined, def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal, def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies, cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined, thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph, lem-successor-cardinal-exists, cor-cardinal-absorption, thm-hessenberg, def-cardinal-arithmetic, lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws, thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting, lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set, def-cardinal, def-axiom-of-choice, def-choice-function, lem-ordinal-basics, lem-ordinal-trichotomy, def-limit-ordinal, lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal, def-equinumerous, def-injection-surjection-bijection]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6049:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:11:justified_by: []
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6050:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:59:[L1] $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) \le \alpha$; for a limit ordinal $\lambda$, $\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$ is an infinite cardinal; every cofinal $C \subseteq \lambda$ has $\operatorname{cf}(\lambda) \le \lvert C\rvert$ ([[thm-cofinality-basics]], [[def-cofinality]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6051:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:61:[L2] $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha)$ is the least length of a cofinal map into $\alpha$, and a strictly increasing cofinal map of that length exists ([[def-cofinality]], [[lem-cofinality-is-well-defined]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6052:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:63:[L3] $\aleph_0 = \omega$; $\aleph_{\alpha+1} = \aleph_\alpha^{+}$ is the least cardinal strictly above $\aleph_\alpha$; $\aleph_\lambda = \bigcup\{\aleph_\beta : \beta \in \lambda\}$ at limits; every $\aleph_\alpha$ is an infinite cardinal and the operation is strictly increasing ([[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]], [[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]], [[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6053:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:65:[L4] Every infinite cardinal is a limit ordinal, and a cardinal is infinite exactly when $\omega \le \kappa$ ([[thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting]], [[def-limit-ordinal]], [[def-cardinal]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6054:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:67:[L5] For an infinite cardinal $\mu$ and a cardinal $\nu$ with $0 \ne \nu \le \mu$, $\mu \otimes \nu = \mu$ ([[cor-cardinal-absorption]], [[thm-hessenberg]], [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6055:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:69:[L6] For cardinals $\kappa \le \lambda$ iff $\kappa \preceq \lambda$; $A \preceq B$ with both well-orderable gives $\lvert A\rvert \le \lvert B\rvert$; $\otimes$ is monotone ([[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6056:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:71:[L7] $\lvert X\rvert$ is the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$, satisfies $X \approx \lvert X\rvert$ and $\lvert \alpha\rvert \le \alpha$, and equals $\alpha$ exactly when $\alpha$ is a cardinal ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]], [[def-equinumerous]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6057:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:73:[L8] Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-choice-function]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6058:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:75:[L9] Ordinals satisfy trichotomy, $\alpha \subseteq \beta$ iff $\alpha \in \beta$ or $\alpha = \beta$, the union of a set of ordinals is its least upper bound, every nonempty set of ordinals has an $\in$-least element, $\omega$ is the least limit ordinal, and every strictly increasing map of ordinals is injective ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]], [[lem-ordinal-basics]], [[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]], [[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6059:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:77:[L10] Every infinite cardinal is $\aleph_\alpha$ for exactly one ordinal $\alpha$ ([[thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6060:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:83:1.1 Claim (a): $\omega$ is a limit ordinal by [L9], so $\operatorname{cf}(\omega)$ is an infinite cardinal by [L1], hence $\omega \le \operatorname{cf}(\omega)$ by [L4]; and $\operatorname{cf}(\omega) \le \omega$ by [L1], so $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_0) = \aleph_0$. [L1, L3, L4, L9]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6061:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:85:1.2 The set $C = \{\aleph_n : n \in \omega\}$ exists by Replacement, is contained in $\aleph_\omega$ and is cofinal in it, since $\aleph_\omega = \bigcup C$ by [L3] means every $\zeta \in \aleph_\omega$ lies in some $\aleph_n$ and hence satisfies $\zeta \le \aleph_n$; moreover $n \mapsto \aleph_n$ is injective by the strict increase in [L3], so $C \approx \omega$ and $\lvert C\rvert = \aleph_0$ by [L7]. [L3, L7, L9]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6062:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:87:1.3 Setting up claim (b): let $\lambda = \aleph_\alpha$, $\kappa = \aleph_{\alpha+1}$, and suppose $\beta = \operatorname{cf}(\kappa) < \kappa$, with $g : \beta \to \kappa$ strictly increasing and cofinal by [L2]; then $\kappa$ is a limit ordinal by [L4], so $\beta$ is an infinite cardinal by [L1], and $\beta \le \lambda$ because $\beta$ is a cardinal below the least cardinal strictly above $\lambda$ ([L3]); moreover $\kappa = \bigcup\{g(\xi) : \xi \in \beta\}$, since for $\zeta \in \kappa$ the ordinal $\zeta \cup \{\zeta\}$ also lies in $\kappa$ and is $\le g(\xi)$ for some $\xi$, putting $\zeta \in g(\xi)$. [L1, L2, L3, L4, L9]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6063:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:89:2.1 Claim (c): step 1.2 and [L1] give $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\omega) \le \aleph_0$; and $\aleph_\omega$ is an infinite cardinal, hence a limit ordinal by [L4], so $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\omega)$ is an infinite cardinal and $\aleph_0 \le \operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\omega)$ by [L1] and [L4]; therefore $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\omega) = \aleph_0 < \aleph_\omega$ by the strict increase in [L3], and $\aleph_\omega$ is singular. [step 1.2, L1, L3, L4]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6064:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:91:2.2 Claim (b): each $g(\xi)$ of step 1.3 lies in $\kappa$, so $\lvert g(\xi)\rvert$ is a cardinal below $\kappa$ and hence $\lvert g(\xi)\rvert \le \lambda$ by [L3] and [L7], and the set $I_\xi$ of injections $g(\xi) \to \lambda$ is nonempty; a choice function from [L8] on $\{I_\xi : \xi \in \beta\}$ supplies injections $e_\xi : g(\xi) \to \lambda$ for all $\xi$ at once, and $\zeta \mapsto (\xi_\zeta, e_{\xi_\zeta}(\zeta))$, with $\xi_\zeta$ the $\in$-least $\xi$ having $\zeta \in g(\xi)$, is then an injection $\kappa \to \beta \times \lambda$; so $\kappa \le \beta \otimes \lambda = \lambda$ by [L6] and [L5], contradicting $\lambda < \kappa$, and therefore $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) = \kappa$. [step 1.3, L5, L6, L7, L8, L9]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-f4-cofinality.log:6065:items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:93:3.1 Claim (d) and the conclusion: an infinite cardinal $\kappa < \aleph_\omega$ is $\aleph_\alpha$ for exactly one $\alpha$ by [L10], and $\alpha \in \omega$, since $\omega \le \alpha$ would give $\aleph_\omega \le \aleph_\alpha$ by the strict increase in [L3]; so $\kappa$ is $\aleph_0$, regular by step 1.1, or $\aleph_{n+1}$ for some $n \in \omega$, regular by step 2.2; with step 2.1 this makes $\aleph_\omega$ the least singular infinite cardinal. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 2.2, L3, L9, L10] ∎
