research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7070:    "thm-regularity-of-the-alephs": {
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7074:          "source": "thm-cofinality-basics",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7076:          "quote": "Work in ZF; no choice principle is used. Let $\\operatorname{cf}$ be the\ncofinality of [[def-cofinality]]. Then:\n\n**(a)** $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha) \\le \\alpha$ for every ordinal $\\alpha$\n([[def-ordinal]]);\n\n**(b)** $\\operatorname{cf}(0) = 0$, and $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha + 1) = 1$ for\nevery ordinal $\\alpha$, where $\\alpha + 1 = \\alpha \\cup \\{\\alpha\\}$\n([[def-ordinal-addition]]);\n\n**(c)** for a limit ordinal $\\lambda$ ([[def-limit-ordinal]]),\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$ is an **infinite cardinal** ([[def-cardinal]]) and\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)) = \\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$, so\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$ is a **regular** cardinal;\n\n**(d)** for a limit ordinal $\\lambda$, every cofinal $C \\subseteq \\lambda$\n([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]) satisfies\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda) \\le \\lvert C \\rvert$, and some cofinal subset of\n$\\lambda$ has cardinality exactly $\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$.\n\nClause (c) is what discharges the naming obligation of [[def-cofinality]]:\n\"regular\" is defined through $\\operatorname{cf}$, and it is a theorem, not a\nconvention, that $\\operatorname{cf}$ of a limit ordinal is a cardinal at which\nthe definition can be tested.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7085:          "source": "def-cofinality",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7087:          "quote": "Let $\\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). The **cofinality** of $\\alpha$ is\n\n$$\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha) \\;:=\\; \\text{the least ordinal } \\beta \\text{ for which some } f : \\beta \\to \\alpha \\text{ has cofinal range},$$\n\ncofinal range meaning that $f[\\beta]$ is a cofinal subset of $\\alpha$\n([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]): every $\\zeta \\in \\alpha$ satisfies\n$\\zeta \\le f(\\xi)$ for some $\\xi \\in \\beta$. That such a least ordinal exists, and\nthat a witnessing map of that length may be taken strictly increasing, is\n[[lem-cofinality-is-well-defined]], and both are theorems of ZF. So\n$\\operatorname{cf}$ is defined at every ordinal, without any choice principle.\n\n**Regular and singular.** An **infinite cardinal** $\\kappa$\n([[def-cardinal]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]) is\n\n- **regular** when $\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) = \\kappa$;\n- **singular** when $\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) \\ne \\kappa$.\n\nThe two cases are exhaustive by definition, and by\n[[thm-cofinality-basics]] singular means exactly\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) < \\kappa$, since $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha) \\le \\alpha$\nalways holds.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7096:          "source": "def-cofinality",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7098:          "quote": "Let $\\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). The **cofinality** of $\\alpha$ is\n\n$$\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha) \\;:=\\; \\text{the least ordinal } \\beta \\text{ for which some } f : \\beta \\to \\alpha \\text{ has cofinal range},$$\n\ncofinal range meaning that $f[\\beta]$ is a cofinal subset of $\\alpha$\n([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]): every $\\zeta \\in \\alpha$ satisfies\n$\\zeta \\le f(\\xi)$ for some $\\xi \\in \\beta$. That such a least ordinal exists, and\nthat a witnessing map of that length may be taken strictly increasing, is\n[[lem-cofinality-is-well-defined]], and both are theorems of ZF. So\n$\\operatorname{cf}$ is defined at every ordinal, without any choice principle.\n\n**Regular and singular.** An **infinite cardinal** $\\kappa$\n([[def-cardinal]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]) is\n\n- **regular** when $\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) = \\kappa$;\n- **singular** when $\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) \\ne \\kappa$.\n\nThe two cases are exhaustive by definition, and by\n[[thm-cofinality-basics]] singular means exactly\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) < \\kappa$, since $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha) \\le \\alpha$\nalways holds.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7446:    "cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power": {
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7469:          "source": "def-cofinality",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7471:          "quote": "Let $\\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). The **cofinality** of $\\alpha$ is\n\n$$\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha) \\;:=\\; \\text{the least ordinal } \\beta \\text{ for which some } f : \\beta \\to \\alpha \\text{ has cofinal range},$$\n\ncofinal range meaning that $f[\\beta]$ is a cofinal subset of $\\alpha$\n([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]): every $\\zeta \\in \\alpha$ satisfies\n$\\zeta \\le f(\\xi)$ for some $\\xi \\in \\beta$. That such a least ordinal exists, and\nthat a witnessing map of that length may be taken strictly increasing, is\n[[lem-cofinality-is-well-defined]], and both are theorems of ZF. So\n$\\operatorname{cf}$ is defined at every ordinal, without any choice principle.\n\n**Regular and singular.** An **infinite cardinal** $\\kappa$\n([[def-cardinal]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]) is\n\n- **regular** when $\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) = \\kappa$;\n- **singular** when $\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) \\ne \\kappa$.\n\nThe two cases are exhaustive by definition, and by\n[[thm-cofinality-basics]] singular means exactly\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) < \\kappa$, since $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha) \\le \\alpha$\nalways holds.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7487:          "source": "thm-cofinality-basics",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7489:          "quote": "Work in ZF; no choice principle is used. Let $\\operatorname{cf}$ be the\ncofinality of [[def-cofinality]]. Then:\n\n**(a)** $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha) \\le \\alpha$ for every ordinal $\\alpha$\n([[def-ordinal]]);\n\n**(b)** $\\operatorname{cf}(0) = 0$, and $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha + 1) = 1$ for\nevery ordinal $\\alpha$, where $\\alpha + 1 = \\alpha \\cup \\{\\alpha\\}$\n([[def-ordinal-addition]]);\n\n**(c)** for a limit ordinal $\\lambda$ ([[def-limit-ordinal]]),\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$ is an **infinite cardinal** ([[def-cardinal]]) and\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)) = \\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$, so\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$ is a **regular** cardinal;\n\n**(d)** for a limit ordinal $\\lambda$, every cofinal $C \\subseteq \\lambda$\n([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]) satisfies\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda) \\le \\lvert C \\rvert$, and some cofinal subset of\n$\\lambda$ has cardinality exactly $\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$.\n\nClause (c) is what discharges the naming obligation of [[def-cofinality]]:\n\"regular\" is defined through $\\operatorname{cf}$, and it is a theorem, not a\nconvention, that $\\operatorname{cf}$ of a limit ordinal is a cardinal at which\nthe definition can be tested.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:7758:    "fs-cardinal-exponentiation-is-strictly-monotone-in-the-base": {
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:8042:          "source": "thm-cofinality-basics",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:8044:          "quote": "Work in ZF; no choice principle is used. Let $\\operatorname{cf}$ be the\ncofinality of [[def-cofinality]]. Then:\n\n**(a)** $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha) \\le \\alpha$ for every ordinal $\\alpha$\n([[def-ordinal]]);\n\n**(b)** $\\operatorname{cf}(0) = 0$, and $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha + 1) = 1$ for\nevery ordinal $\\alpha$, where $\\alpha + 1 = \\alpha \\cup \\{\\alpha\\}$\n([[def-ordinal-addition]]);\n\n**(c)** for a limit ordinal $\\lambda$ ([[def-limit-ordinal]]),\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$ is an **infinite cardinal** ([[def-cardinal]]) and\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)) = \\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$, so\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$ is a **regular** cardinal;\n\n**(d)** for a limit ordinal $\\lambda$, every cofinal $C \\subseteq \\lambda$\n([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]) satisfies\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda) \\le \\lvert C \\rvert$, and some cofinal subset of\n$\\lambda$ has cardinality exactly $\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$.\n\nClause (c) is what discharges the naming obligation of [[def-cofinality]]:\n\"regular\" is defined through $\\operatorname{cf}$, and it is a theorem, not a\nconvention, that $\\operatorname{cf}$ of a limit ordinal is a cardinal at which\nthe definition can be tested.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations-apply.log:8051:          "source": "def-cofinality",
