research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:547009:+     "quote": "Let $\\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). The **cofinality** of $\\alpha$ is\n\n$$\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha) \\;:=\\; \\text{the least ordinal } \\beta \\text{ for which some } f : \\beta \\to \\alpha \\text{ has cofinal range},$$\n\ncofinal range meaning that $f[\\beta]$ is a cofinal subset of $\\alpha$\n([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]): every $\\zeta \\in \\alpha$ satisfies\n$\\zeta \\le f(\\xi)$ for some $\\xi \\in \\beta$. That such a least ordinal exists, and\nthat a witnessing map of that length may be taken strictly increasing, is\n[[lem-cofinality-is-well-defined]], and both are theorems of ZF. So\n$\\operatorname{cf}$ is defined at every ordinal, without any choice principle.\n\n**Regular and singular.** An **infinite cardinal** $\\kappa$\n([[def-cardinal]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]) is\n\n- **regular** when $\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) = \\kappa$;\n- **singular** when $\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) \\ne \\kappa$.\n\nThe two cases are exhaustive by definition, and by\n[[thm-cofinality-basics]] singular means exactly\n$\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) < \\kappa$, since $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha) \\le \\alpha$\nalways holds.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:547446:+{"id":"def-cofinality","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.ucla.edu/~marks/notes/set_theory_notes.pdf","https://people.math.harvard.edu/~wboney/fall16/settheory.pdf"],"rationale":"The sourced definition is the least length of a cofinal map. The item uses it for all ordinals, not only limits, and folds regular/singular terminology and the explicit choice-free convention into the definition. Every listed URL was opened through the model-side web fetch route and returned rendered HTML or PDF content (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:547447:+{"id":"thm-cofinality-basics","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.ucla.edu/~marks/notes/set_theory_notes.pdf","https://people.math.harvard.edu/~wboney/fall16/settheory.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give cf(0), successor cofinality, infinite-limit cofinality, regularity of cofinalities, and minimum cofinal-set size. The item bundles those results and adapts the proofs to its all-ordinal definition. Every listed URL was opened through the model-side web fetch route and returned rendered HTML or PDF content (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:547448:+{"id":"thm-regularity-of-the-alephs","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.ucla.edu/~marks/notes/set_theory_notes.pdf","https://people.math.harvard.edu/~wboney/fall16/settheory.pdf"],"rationale":"The literature gives regularity of omega and successor cardinals under choice and singularity of aleph_omega. The item bundles four clauses, isolates the exact AC expenditure, and adds the least-singular conclusion. Every listed URL was opened through the model-side web fetch route and returned rendered HTML or PDF content (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:547451:+{"id":"cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.colorado.edu/~kearnes/Teaching/Courses/F25/cardinals_arithmetic.pdf","https://math.ucla.edu/~marks/notes/set_theory_notes.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources prove kappa<kappa^cf(kappa) and cf(2^kappa)>kappa. The item combines those results with the continuum specialization and derives them through its family-form König theorem. Every listed URL was opened through the model-side web fetch route and returned rendered HTML or PDF content (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:547454:+{"id":"fs-every-aleph-is-regular","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.harvard.edu/~wboney/fall16/settheory.pdf","https://math.ucla.edu/~marks/notes/set_theory_notes.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources state regularity of successor cardinals under choice and singularity of aleph_omega with countable cofinality. The item reframes that standard boundary as the false universal claim that every aleph is regular. Every listed URL was opened through the model-side web fetch route and returned rendered HTML or PDF content (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-foundations.log:547455:+{"id":"fs-cardinal-exponentiation-is-strictly-monotone-in-the-base","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.harvard.edu/~wboney/fall16/settheory.pdf","https://math.colorado.edu/~kearnes/Teaching/Courses/F25/cardinals_arithmetic.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the exponent-collapse law 2<=kappa<=lambda with lambda infinite implies kappa^lambda=2^lambda, which yields the displayed aleph_0/aleph_1 witness. The item reframes it as failure of a false strict base-monotonicity claim. Every listed URL was opened through the model-side web fetch route and returned rendered HTML or PDF content (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
